{
  "messages": [
    {"role": "user", "content": "Refer to lines 6–7: 'The first time … roar and charged.'\n\nHow do these lines influence your attitude to Richard Parker at this stage in the novel? Substantiate your response. (2)"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Diagnostic turn. The question asks for attitude + substantiation. I need to guide the learner to notice the specific verbs and physical actions in lines 6-7 before naming the attitude. I'll use a real-world SA analogy (a wild animal encounter) to make the feeling concrete, then ask one small question.", "content": "Let's look at what Richard Parker actually *does* in those two lines. He doesn't just sit there — he bares his teeth, rotates his ears, vomits a guttural roar, and charges. Then a full-clawed paw cuffs the shield.\n\nThink about it like this: imagine you're walking through a township after dark and you round a corner and come face-to-face with a full-grown leopard that's already showing its teeth and ready to pounce. What's your first physical reaction? Your heart races, you freeze, you feel small, right?\n\nSo here's my question for you: based on those actions, what ONE word would you use to describe how the reader is meant to feel toward Richard Parker at this point?"},
    {"role": "user", "content": "I think the reader feels scared because the tiger is so aggressive and dangerous. He could kill Pi."},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner has the right idea (fear/scared) but the response is informal and lacks the second mark — substantiation needs to link specific textual evidence to the attitude. Also 'tiger' is a slip (Richard Parker is a Bengal tiger, so that's actually correct, but the phrasing is loose). I should validate the core idea, then push for the second mark by asking them to connect the evidence. The coaching profile is Deep-Dive Socratic, so I continue diagnosing before giving the full answer.", "content": "Yes — fear is exactly the right starting point. You've identified the attitude, which is the first mark.\n\nNow, to earn the second mark, the marker wants you to *substantiate* — that means pointing to the specific evidence in the text that makes you feel that way, and explaining why it matters. Remember the memo says these lines remind the reader that Richard Parker is a dangerous predator who must be treated with caution.\n\nSo let me ask: which TWO things from lines 6–7 make you feel that fear? Is it the roar, the charge, the claws, or the fact that Pi gets knocked off the boat? Pick the strongest evidence and tell me how it shows Richard Parker is a lethal threat."}
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "analogy_real_world",
    "final_answer": "These lines evoke fear and healthy respect for Richard Parker, reminding the reader that he is a formidable apex predator whose sheer power and ferocity pose an immediate, lethal threat.",
    "verify_code": "",
    "techniques": ["annotate_text", "skim_and_scan"]
  }
}

{
  "messages": [
